{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "לכל דבר נקי", :translit "lechol davar naki", :gloss "for every matter [he shall be] free"} {:he "הכל יודעין כלה למה היא נכנסת לחופה", :translit "hakol yod'in kalah lamah hi nikneiset la-chupah", :gloss "everyone knows a bride why she enters the canopy"} {:he "לכל דבר נקי יהיה לביתו", :translit "lechol davar naki yihyeh le-veito", :gloss "for every matter [he shall be] free for his house"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Glosses 'naki' as free of sin in speech: marital intimacy permits what would otherwise be improper speech."} {:kind :other, :note "Second interpretation: marrying a woman earns forgiveness for all sins."}], :frame {:text "The phrase 'everyone knows why a bride enters the wedding canopy' appears in Talmudic discussion where it is stated euphemistically to indicate that marital relations are universally understood, as in Ketubot 3a. The idea that one who marries is forgiven for all his sins is found in Talmud Yevamot 63b, where Rava teaches that one who marries, sins are forgiven him, derived from the word 'naki' (free).", :sources ["Ketubot 3a" "Yevamot 63b"]}} {:quotes [{:he "נקי יהיה לביתו שנה", :translit "naki yihyeh le-veito shanah", :gloss "[he shall be] free for his house one year"} {:he "שם של ד' אותיות", :translit "shem shel dalet otot", :gloss "Name of four letters"} {:he "ושמח", :translit "ve-samach", :gloss "and rejoice"} {:he "כי יקח", :translit "ki yikach", :gloss "when [a man] takes"} {:he "קיחה", :translit "kikchah", :gloss "taking"} {:he "אשתו אשר לקח", :translit "ishto asher lakach", :gloss "his wife whom he has taken"} {:he "לא יחבול", :translit "lo yachbol", :gloss "he shall not take a pledge"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "נקי יהיה לביתו שנה", :spells "ס ת ת"} {:kind :other, :note "Same letters as 'sefer Torah' (ס\"ת) to signal the four-letter Name of God as witness to marriage."} {:kind :other, :note "Wordplay on לא יחבול / לא יתבול (not take a pledge / not withhold relations)."}], :frame {:text "The obligation of a groom to rejoice with his wife for one full year, during which he is exempt from certain public obligations, is established in Sotah 44a and Mishnah Sotah 8:1. The obligation for conjugal relations throughout that year, except for Yom HaKippurim, is discussed in Talmud Ketubot 61b. The statement that one who marries a woman in his youth should marry a woman in his old age derives from the reading of the phrase 'his wife whom he has taken' (Deuteronomy 24:5). The teaching that a man's wife is his pledge, and that she may die on account of the sin of vows, is found in Talmud Shabbat 32b, where Rava states that for the sin of vows a man's wife dies.", :sources ["Sotah 44a" "Sotah 8:1" "Ketubot 61b" "Shabbat 32b" "Deuteronomy 24:5"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Glosses 'naki' as free of sin in speech: marital intimacy permits what would otherwise be improper speech.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Second interpretation: marrying a woman earns forgiveness for all sins.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "נקי יהיה לביתו שנה", :spells "ס ת ת", :letters ["נ" "י" "ל" "ש"], :by :selah, :order :mismatch} {:kind :other, :note "Same letters as 'sefer Torah' (ס\"ת) to signal the four-letter Name of God as witness to marriage.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Wordplay on לא יחבול / לא יתבול (not take a pledge / not withhold relations).", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:39:26.214191091Z", :model "glm-5.2"}